    <section id="overview"><h2>Modern GenAI: Vision Transformers, RAG and efficient adaptation</h2>
<p>The supplied Week 11–12 resources connect three ideas: Vision Transformers (ViTs) treat image patches like a token sequence; Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) gives a language model external retrieved context; and parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT), including LoRA, adapts large models without updating every base-model parameter.</p><p>These are different tools for different problems: ViT is an image architecture, RAG is a retrieval-plus-generation pipeline, and LoRA is a model-adaptation method.</p>
</section>
<section id="vit"><h2>Vision Transformer: images as patch tokens</h2>
<p>A ViT splits an image into fixed-size patches, flattens each patch and projects it into an embedding. A positional embedding tells the Transformer where each patch came from; a learned class token (in common ViT designs) can aggregate information for image classification.</p><div class="eq">\(N=\frac{H}{P}\times\frac{W}{P}\)</div><p>For a 224×224 image and 16×16 patches, there are 14×14=196 patches. If the image has 3 channels, each flattened patch contains 16×16×3=768 values before projection. A convolution with kernel size 16 and stride 16 can perform patch extraction and projection in one operation.</p><p>CNNs have a useful local inductive bias and can perform well with limited data. ViTs use global self-attention and often benefit from large datasets or pretraining.</p>
</section>
<section id="rag"><h2>Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG)</h2>
<p>RAG combines a retrieval system with a language model. Instead of asking the model to rely only on parameters learned during training, the system retrieves relevant external passages and supplies them as context for generation.</p><ol><li><strong>Ingest:</strong> load documents and extract text.</li><li><strong>Chunk:</strong> split long documents into manageable, often overlapping passages.</li><li><strong>Embed:</strong> convert chunks to vectors.</li><li><strong>Index:</strong> store vectors and metadata in a searchable index.</li><li><strong>Retrieve:</strong> embed the query and find relevant chunks.</li><li><strong>Generate:</strong> place retrieved context and the question into a prompt, then ask the model to answer.</li></ol><p>RAG can improve freshness and traceability, but it does not guarantee correctness. Retrieval can miss relevant evidence, and a model can misinterpret retrieved text. Use source citations, relevant chunking, access controls and evaluation.</p>
</section>
<section id="lora"><h2>PEFT and LoRA fine-tuning</h2>
<p>Full fine-tuning updates the model's original parameters, which can be expensive for large language models. PEFT methods adapt a model while training a smaller subset of parameters. LoRA freezes the original weight matrix \(W\) and learns a low-rank update:</p><div class="eq">\(W\prime=W+\Delta W,\qquad\Delta W=BA\)</div><p>Here A and B are smaller trainable matrices, often with rank much lower than the original matrix dimensions. This reduces trainable parameter count and optimizer memory. LoRA adapters can be saved separately and loaded alongside the base model.</p><p>Fine-tuning still requires a clean, representative dataset, appropriate evaluation and checks for overfitting. PEFT reduces cost; it does not automatically improve the data or guarantee better answers.</p>
</section>
<section id="finetune-workflow"><h2>Fine-tuning workflow: from documents to a trained adapter</h2>
<p>The supplied live-session resources demonstrate a practical workflow for adapting an open model using question-answer examples derived from PDF material. The quality of the examples is as important as the optimizer settings.</p>
<ol><li><strong>Extract:</strong> read PDF text and inspect the extraction; tables, columns and page headers may be scrambled.</li><li><strong>Clean:</strong> remove repeated headers/footers, repair line breaks, and preserve important equations and context.</li><li><strong>Create QA pairs:</strong> write a question that can be answered from the source and a faithful answer. Avoid unsupported details and duplicate questions.</li><li><strong>Format:</strong> serialize examples into the instruction/chat format expected by the model and tokenizer.</li><li><strong>Split:</strong> separate training and validation examples before tuning. Near-duplicate examples across splits can inflate the validation score.</li><li><strong>Train with PEFT/LoRA:</strong> freeze the base weights and update the adapter parameters using the chosen supervised fine-tuning objective.</li><li><strong>Monitor and evaluate:</strong> track training and validation loss, inspect generated answers, and compare with the untuned base model on held-out examples.</li></ol>
<p><strong>TensorBoard</strong> can display training curves and other logged metrics. A falling training loss alone does not prove better factual accuracy: also test held-out questions, source faithfulness, format compliance and regressions on unrelated tasks.</p>
<p>Fine-tuning changes model behaviour through learned parameters; RAG instead provides retrieved source text at inference time. They can be combined, but they solve different problems.</p></section><section id="prompting"><h2>Prompting, inference and evaluation</h2>
<p>A prompt specifies the task, relevant context, constraints and desired output format. Clear prompts reduce ambiguity but cannot guarantee factual correctness. Few-shot prompting supplies examples; retrieval can supply task-specific evidence; fine-tuning changes model behaviour through training.</p><p>Distinguish the following:</p><ul><li><strong>Prompting:</strong> changes the input at inference time; model weights remain fixed.</li><li><strong>RAG:</strong> retrieves external information and adds it to the prompt.</li><li><strong>Fine-tuning:</strong> updates some or all model parameters using examples.</li><li><strong>LoRA:</strong> a parameter-efficient fine-tuning method.</li></ul><p>When preparing a QA dataset from PDFs, verify extraction quality, remove duplicate/noisy examples, keep training and evaluation examples separate, and ensure the evaluation set measures the intended task.</p>
</section>
<section id="questions"><h2>Week 12 questions and solutions</h2>
<p>Original practice based on the supplied ViT, RAG and LLM fine-tuning resources.</p><details class="question"><summary>Q1. A 224×224 image is split into 16×16 patches. How many patches are produced?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> 196 patches</p><p>There are 224/16=14 patches per side, so 14×14=196 patches.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q2. What does a positional embedding provide to a Vision Transformer?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Information about where each patch occurs in the image sequence.</p><p>Self-attention mixes patch representations; positional information helps distinguish their spatial arrangement.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q3. What is the purpose of retrieval in RAG?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Find relevant external passages to include as context for generation.</p><p>The generator can condition its answer on retrieved material rather than relying only on parametric memory.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q4. What is one limitation of RAG?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Retrieved passages may be irrelevant or incomplete, and the model may still misinterpret them.</p><p>RAG improves access to evidence but does not guarantee retrieval or answer correctness.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q5. What does LoRA train?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Low-rank adapter matrices that represent a weight update.</p><p>The base weights are typically frozen while a smaller number of adapter parameters are optimized.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q6. Does prompting alone update model weights?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No</p><p>Prompting changes the input supplied at inference time; fine-tuning is a training process that updates parameters.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q7. How does full fine-tuning differ from typical LoRA fine-tuning?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Full fine-tuning updates base-model parameters; LoRA usually freezes them and trains low-rank adapters.</p><p>The LoRA approach reduces trainable parameter and optimizer-memory requirements.</p></div></details><details class="question"><summary>Q8. What is a common RAG pipeline order?</summary><div class="answer"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Load and chunk documents → embed/index chunks → retrieve for a query → generate from retrieved context.</p><p>The retrieval stage selects context before generation; the model cannot use a passage that was never retrieved or supplied.</p></div></details>
</section>

